'''''Ctenanthe burle-marxii''''', commonly known as the '''fishbone prayer plant''', is a species of plant in the genus ''[[Ctenanthe]]'' native to Brazil.<ref>{{citation |url=https://wcsp.science.kew.org/namedetail.do?name_id=235150|title=Ctenanthe burle-marxii H.Kenn., Canad. J. Bot. 60: 2367 (1982). |work=World Checklist of Selected Plant Families}}</ref> Its common name derives from the alternating pattern of stripes on its oval, pale green leaves, while its scientific name is honor of landscape architect [[Roberto Burle Marx]]. Similar to other plants in the family [[Marantaceae]], it also has a propensity to close up its leaves at night, which is why it is also called a "prayer plant" like ''[[Maranta leuconeura]]''. It grows well as a form of tropical ground cover, often in shade.<ref>{{citation|author1=Fred Donald Rauch|author2=Paul R. Weissich|title=Plants for Tropical Landscapes: A Gardener's Guide|year=2000|publisher=University of Hawaii Press|isbn=978-0-8248-2034-3|page=98}}</ref>

